.TH TIMED 8
.SH NAME
timed \- wall clock, SNTP discipline and time status
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timed
.RB [ -d
.IR dir ]
.RB [ -s
.IR server ]
.RB [ -i
.IR seconds ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B timed
owns the wall clock.  It sets a coarse time at boot and then, when
servers are configured, disciplines it with SNTP.
.PP
There are two clocks.  The wall clock is
.BR /dev/time :
microseconds since the epoch, writable by the host owner, and shared by
every namespace.  The monotonic clock is
.BR millisec (2)
in the kernel (milliseconds since boot) and
.BR clock_gettime (2)
under the emulator; it is never set, and timeouts should use it.
.PP
At start,
.B timed
reads
.BR /dev/time .
If it is before 2020 it tries
.BR /n/host/time ,
the epoch the 9vm host writes into the 9P tree (the guest has no RTC).
Otherwise the clock is accepted as a coarse host source.
.PP
Servers come from
.B -s
options or from
.BR /lib/ndb/time ,
one
.B sntp=\fIhost\fP
line per server.  After a successful exchange it computes
offset and round-trip delay from the four timestamps and steps
.B /dev/time
when the offset exceeds one second, or when the clock has not yet been
verified by SNTP.  Smaller offsets are reported but not corrected.
.PP
The service republishes
.BI dir /status
after each sync and every interval:
.PP
.RS
.EX
source=host|sntp
synced=1
verified=0|1
server=S
offset_us=N
delay_us=N
stratum=N
last=unixseconds
now=microseconds
mono=milliseconds
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B now
and
.B mono
are written when the file is published, not live; use
.B /dev/time
and
.B millisec
for the current values.
.SH OPTIONS
.TF "-i seconds"
.TP
.BI -d " dir"
Publish directory (default
.BR /n/time ).
.TP
.BI -s " server"
Add an SNTP server; may be repeated.
.TP
.BI -i " seconds"
Seconds between syncs (default 900).
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/ndb/time
.TP
.B /dev/time
wall clock, microseconds since the epoch
.TP
.B /n/host/time
host epoch seconds on 9vm
.TP
.B /lib/ndb/time
.B sntp=
lines
.TP
.B /n/time/status
last published state
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/timed.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR millisec (2),
.IR sys (2)
.SH BUGS
Offsets are stepped, not slewed, so a later correction can move the
wall clock backwards.  Leap seconds, NTP authentication and server
selection by stratum are not implemented; SNTP replies are accepted
from the first configured server that answers.  A reply is checked for
the client's origin timestamp but the server is not authenticated,
so this is only as trustworthy as the network path.
